  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Vallamkondu Ramanaiah vs The State Of Andhra Pradesh on 11 September, 2019 Author: U. Durga Prasad Rao Bench: U. Durga Prasad Rao Hon'Ble Sri Justice U. Durga Prasad Rao Criminal Petition No.5146 Of 2019 Order:",
    "facts": "The petitioners/A1 and A2 seek to quash the proceedings against them in P.R.C.No.39 of 2019 on the file of learned Junior Civil Judge - cum - First Additional Judicial Magistrate of First Class, Badvel, which was taken cognizance under Sections 188, 272, 273 and 328 of IPC and Sections 57 and 58 r/w 26, 30 of Food Safety and Standards Act (for short, 'FSS Act'). 2. The allegations against the petitioners briefly are that on 04.07.2019 at 08.30 p.m., when the complainant along with his staff conducting a vehicle check at Rajupalem Bus Stop on Badvel - Siddavaram road, seized auto bearing No.AP 04UB 1362 and on enquiry, the accused revealed that they have been involved in selling of banned tobacco products for earning money, under the cover of mediator's report and registered the same as a case in Crime No.118 of 2019 for the offences stated supra. 3. Learned counsel for petitioners while denying the FIR allegations would submit that even if the prosecution case is accepted to be true, tobacco products would not fall within the definition of Section 3(j) of FSS Act and therefore, the Police are not authorized to register the crime, investigate and file charge sheet and therefore, registration of the FIR is contrary to the provisions of FSS Act. He relied upon the order of this Court in Crl.P.Nos.3731 of 2018 and batch, dated 27.08.2018.",
    "arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "4. So far as the offence under Section 188 of IPC is concerned, as per Section 195(1)(a)(i) of Cr.P.C, no Court shall take cognizance of the said offence except on the complaint in writing of that Court or by such Officer of the Court as that Court may authorise in writing in this behalf, or of some other Court to which that Court is subordinate."
  },
